# Single source of truth for this cluster's DNS identity.
#
# Copy to cluster-domain.env and fill in the values (the real file is
# gitignored -- it holds a live cluster's identity, which changes on rebuild):
#
#   cp cluster-domain.env.template cluster-domain.env
#
# Why this file exists: the domain PREFIX survives a rebuild (it is passed to
# `rosa create cluster --domain-prefix`), but Red Hat assigns a fresh shard
# segment after it on every create. Two values in the gitops tree embed that
# domain, and a stale value in either one is severe:
#
#   * gitops/bootstrap/cilium/values.yaml  k8sServiceHost
#       -> Cilium cannot reach the apiserver; with kubeProxyReplacement there is
#          no service-IP fallback, so every node goes NotReady.
#   * Harbor's Route host + externalURL
#       -> every image pull in the cluster fails, because the registry hostname
#          no longer resolves or no longer matches the wildcard certificate.
#
# Both are rewritten BY KEY (never by old value, so it is idempotent from any
# starting state) by scripts/set-cluster-domain.sh. Re-derive after a rebuild:
#   terraform -chdir=terraform/2-cluster output -raw cluster_domain
CLUSTER_DOMAIN=<cluster-id>.<shard>.p3.openshiftapps.com
APPS_DOMAIN=apps.rosa.<cluster-id>.<shard>.p3.openshiftapps.com
# The Harbor UI Route, on the cluster apps domain -- rebuild-variable.
HARBOR_UI_HOST=harbor.apps.rosa.<cluster-id>.<shard>.p3.openshiftapps.com

# The REGISTRY endpoint every image reference uses. Deliberately NOT on the
# cluster apps domain: it is your own domain, resolved inside the VPC by a
# Route53 private zone to the internal ELB, so it survives a rebuild untouched
# and the image references never need rewriting. Kept here for reference and
# for --check; set-cluster-domain.sh verifies rather than rewrites it.
HARBOR_REGISTRY_HOST=harbor.openshift.example.com
